WEBVTT

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
♪

00:35.000 --> 00:40.000
♪

00:40.000 --> 00:45.000
♪

00:45.000 --> 00:50.000
♪

00:50.000 --> 00:55.000
♪

00:55.000 --> 01:00.000
♪

01:00.000 --> 01:05.000
♪

01:05.000 --> 01:10.000
♪

01:10.000 --> 01:15.000
♪

01:15.000 --> 01:17.000
♪

01:17.000 --> 01:20.000
Shady Rest Hotel, is that the place your mother runs?

01:20.000 --> 01:22.000
My goodness, are we here already?

01:22.000 --> 01:25.000
We're here already, and we've only been walking for four hours.

01:25.000 --> 01:29.000
I've enjoyed listening to you so much, I haven't even been conscious of walking.

01:29.000 --> 01:31.000
Yeah, well I have.

01:31.000 --> 01:35.000
Well, come on up and take a hot bath and get some food.

01:35.000 --> 01:36.000
You feel lots better.

01:36.000 --> 01:40.000
No, I'm just going to rest here for a while, then I'm going to move on.

01:40.000 --> 01:44.000
But Alan, I wanted you to meet my mom and my sisters and my Uncle Joe.

01:44.000 --> 01:46.000
No, not that family bit.

01:46.000 --> 01:50.000
I mean that warm, friendly, small talk just turns me right off.

01:50.000 --> 01:52.000
You mean...

01:52.000 --> 01:53.000
I mean it's so insincere.

01:53.000 --> 01:57.000
People ask you how you are, but they don't listen to what you say.

01:57.000 --> 01:59.000
Why?

01:59.000 --> 02:01.000
Because they don't care.

02:01.000 --> 02:03.000
My family would care?

02:03.000 --> 02:05.000
You think so, huh?

02:05.000 --> 02:10.000
You get home from school, they probably say, um, how was school, right?

02:10.000 --> 02:11.000
Something like that.

02:11.000 --> 02:17.000
Well, I bet you that if you say lousy, they'll say, oh, that's fine, let's eat.

02:17.000 --> 02:21.000
Oh, baby, don't you see how confused you are?

02:21.000 --> 02:29.000
Yes, Alan, I am confused, but I want to find the truth as you have found it.

02:29.000 --> 02:33.000
Please stay and help me.

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
Oh, no, baby, you're too soft.

02:36.000 --> 02:39.000
You're too tender to face truth.

02:39.000 --> 02:44.000
You're a dove, I'm an eagle, I'm screaming at life, I'm longing just to tear it wide open.

02:44.000 --> 02:48.000
Oh, please, Alan, please stay at least for a while.

02:48.000 --> 02:51.000
Why, you're such a nice kid, you're bright too.

02:51.000 --> 02:54.000
I just want to rest here for a while and then I'm going to be moving on.

02:54.000 --> 02:57.000
Well, my mom's a wonderful cook, Alan, and I know you must be hungry.

02:57.000 --> 03:01.000
And I promise you, you won't have to make any small talk with the family.

03:01.000 --> 03:03.000
You can just eat and everyone will be quiet.

03:03.000 --> 03:05.000
Will it make you happy, kid?

03:05.000 --> 03:06.000
Yes, Alan.

03:06.000 --> 03:09.000
Listen, you wait here and I'll run up and warn everybody.

03:09.000 --> 03:11.000
I mean that you don't want to be bored and all.

03:11.000 --> 03:18.000
Okay, listen, you fly on, dove, maybe the eagle will be here when you get back and maybe not.

03:18.000 --> 03:21.000
Oh, please be here, Alan, please.

03:21.000 --> 03:25.000
He says he's from New York?

03:25.000 --> 03:26.000
New York City.

03:26.000 --> 03:27.000
He's a writer and a poet.

03:27.000 --> 03:29.000
And you say he's good looking, huh?

03:29.000 --> 03:31.000
Oh, he's handsome and so intellectual.

03:31.000 --> 03:33.000
And you left him sitting down at the track?

03:33.000 --> 03:34.000
Well, he's resting.

03:34.000 --> 03:36.000
We walked all the way from Hooterville.

03:36.000 --> 03:39.000
You walked all the way from Hooterville?

03:39.000 --> 03:43.000
Not really, Mom. I floated, I flew, I danced.

03:43.000 --> 03:45.000
Oh, Mom, he's so brilliant.

03:45.000 --> 03:47.000
Where are you going, Billy Joe?

03:47.000 --> 03:49.000
Well, I just thought I'd freshen up a little bit.

03:49.000 --> 03:51.000
We want to make a good impression, don't we?

03:51.000 --> 03:53.000
We sure do, Billy.

03:53.000 --> 03:57.000
Oh, Mom, Alan's the first boy I've ever met that I can really communicate with.

03:57.000 --> 04:00.000
Oh, he knows everything, he's read everything.

04:00.000 --> 04:06.000
He's so deep and so alive, so sensitive and so profound.

04:06.000 --> 04:08.000
And you met him in Hooterville?

04:08.000 --> 04:11.000
Uh-huh, at the library on my way to school this morning.

04:11.000 --> 04:16.000
Bobby Joe, come over here and sit down and tell me everything, right from the beginning.

04:16.000 --> 04:17.000
Okay.

04:17.000 --> 04:18.000
Hi, Bobby. How are things at school today?

04:18.000 --> 04:20.000
Oh, just lousy.

04:20.000 --> 04:22.000
That's fine. Let's eat, Kate.

04:22.000 --> 04:25.000
You see, we just don't listen to each other.

04:25.000 --> 04:26.000
We're just not alive.

04:26.000 --> 04:28.000
Well, I'm alive and I'm hungry.

04:28.000 --> 04:30.000
We're all hungry, Uncle Joe.

04:30.000 --> 04:34.000
We're hungry to know who we are, where we are, and why we're here.

04:34.000 --> 04:36.000
Well, speaking for myself, I'm Joe Carson.

04:36.000 --> 04:39.000
I'm in the kitchen. I'm in here to eat.

04:39.000 --> 04:41.000
Oh, no wonder Alan's so bitter.

04:41.000 --> 04:59.000
♪

04:59.000 --> 05:01.000
Oh, hello.

05:01.000 --> 05:03.000
I didn't know you were here.

05:03.000 --> 05:06.000
Yes, you did. That's why you came down here.

05:06.000 --> 05:11.000
Well, come to think of it, my sister did mention something.

05:11.000 --> 05:14.000
Are you the boy from New York City?

05:14.000 --> 05:16.000
Greenwich Village.

05:16.000 --> 05:18.000
Oh, Bobby Joe said New York.

05:18.000 --> 05:24.000
You know, she never gets anything right.

05:24.000 --> 05:27.000
If you were to move your feet, I could sit down.

05:27.000 --> 05:30.000
I know.

05:30.000 --> 05:32.000
I guess you are tired.

05:32.000 --> 05:34.000
Yeah, I'm tired.

05:34.000 --> 05:37.000
Of painted-up, empty-headed chicks like you.

05:37.000 --> 05:38.000
What?

05:38.000 --> 05:40.000
You're all alike.

05:40.000 --> 05:44.000
And why do you have to keep proving to yourself that you're a woman and don't you know?

05:44.000 --> 05:47.000
I know that you're the rudest man that I've ever met.

05:47.000 --> 05:50.000
Bugs you, doesn't it?

05:50.000 --> 05:52.000
Hi, Billy. Did you meet Alan?

05:52.000 --> 05:53.000
Yes.

05:53.000 --> 05:58.000
Bobby, you take my advice and don't fall for him because he's already in love.

05:58.000 --> 05:59.000
He is?

05:59.000 --> 06:01.000
Yes, with himself.

06:01.000 --> 06:07.000
You've got against the boy. You haven't even met him.

06:07.000 --> 06:12.000
Well, A, I don't trust big city fellows, especially from New York.

06:12.000 --> 06:16.000
And B, I don't trust writers, especially poets.

06:16.000 --> 06:20.000
And C, when you put the two together, you're going to have a lot of trouble.

06:20.000 --> 06:28.000
Bobby Joe says he's the first boy with whom she ever had an intellectual rapport.

06:28.000 --> 06:31.000
Now you see already you've got trouble.

06:31.000 --> 06:35.000
Uncle Joe, an intellectual rapport means a meeting of the minds.

06:35.000 --> 06:38.000
Kate, Kate, you don't know poets like I do.

06:38.000 --> 06:40.000
Oh, where'd you ever learn about poets?

06:40.000 --> 06:42.000
From reading their poetry, that's where.

06:42.000 --> 06:43.000
Like what?

06:43.000 --> 06:46.000
Like this. There was a young girl from Duluth.

06:46.000 --> 06:50.000
Uncle Joe, I don't know poetry.

06:50.000 --> 06:52.000
What do you mean it ain't? It rhymes.

06:52.000 --> 06:54.000
Last line goes...

06:54.000 --> 07:02.000
I don't want to hear it, and I also don't want to hear any more talk against Bobby Joe's poet friend.

07:02.000 --> 07:05.000
I understand exactly how she feels.

07:05.000 --> 07:11.000
When I was a girl, I was in love with Longfellow, Whittier, Byron, Keats, Shelley.

07:11.000 --> 07:13.000
All at the same time.

07:13.000 --> 07:20.000
Yeah, but one big city fellow can be more dangerous than them five local boys put together.

07:20.000 --> 07:21.000
Mom?

07:21.000 --> 07:25.000
In here, Bobby Joe.

07:25.000 --> 07:29.000
Mom, Uncle Joe, I'd like you to meet Alan Landman.

07:29.000 --> 07:33.000
How do you do, Mr. Landman? It's an honor to have you here.

07:33.000 --> 07:35.000
What kind of poem do you write, Alan?

07:35.000 --> 07:38.000
My poetry is...

07:38.000 --> 07:41.000
A Cry of Anguish in the Tortured Night.

07:41.000 --> 07:45.000
Oh, you write jingles for them indigestion commercials.

07:45.000 --> 07:46.000
Uncle Joe!

07:46.000 --> 07:51.000
Oh, no, no, I'm sure Mr. Landman is way above anything like commercial jingles.

07:51.000 --> 07:53.000
Of course he is.

07:53.000 --> 07:58.000
My poetry is a scream of protest.

07:58.000 --> 08:00.000
I sound the alarm.

08:00.000 --> 08:04.000
I hang the bell on the cat.

08:04.000 --> 08:10.000
I shout in the atrophied ear of a sleeping America.

08:10.000 --> 08:12.000
Isn't that marvelous?

08:12.000 --> 08:14.000
It's wonderful.

08:14.000 --> 08:15.000
You like it?

08:15.000 --> 08:20.000
It's absolutely fascinating, inspirational.

08:20.000 --> 08:22.000
You didn't understand a word I said.

08:22.000 --> 08:23.000
Not a word.

08:23.000 --> 08:27.000
The End

08:41.000 --> 08:44.000
What a meal! That Dutch Alpapa was laughing good.

08:44.000 --> 08:46.000
Kate really outdone herself tonight.

08:46.000 --> 08:47.000
Sure you.

08:47.000 --> 08:51.000
You keep on eating like that, you're going to have to back up to a door to knock on it.

08:51.000 --> 08:54.000
Oh, Uncle Joe, stop teasing Charlie. He's not so fat.

08:54.000 --> 08:56.000
I've seen people a lot fatter.

08:56.000 --> 08:58.000
Yeah, me too, but I had to buy a ticket.

08:58.000 --> 09:02.000
Mom, do I have to take part in that silly poetry reading?

09:02.000 --> 09:05.000
Oh, of course not, dear. You can wash the dishes.

09:05.000 --> 09:09.000
Well, you know, I'd rather wash the dishes than listen to you-know-who.

09:11.000 --> 09:14.000
Now that Mr. Landman is here, we can start the poetry discussion.

09:14.000 --> 09:16.000
Be seated, everybody.

09:16.000 --> 09:22.000
Now, I thought it'd be kind of nice if each of us recited a couple of lines from our favorite poem.

09:22.000 --> 09:28.000
And then I'm sure that Mr. Landman will favor us with one of his original compositions.

09:32.000 --> 09:34.000
Well, you start it off, Kate. You're already on your feet.

09:34.000 --> 09:37.000
It'll take Charlie an hour to get to his.

09:37.000 --> 09:41.000
This is from the Rubiat of Omar Khayyam.

09:41.000 --> 09:49.000
Here with a loaf of bread beneath a bough, a flask of wine, a book of verse,

09:49.000 --> 09:57.000
and thou beside me singing in the wilderness, and wilderness is paradise-y now.

10:00.000 --> 10:03.000
Ain't that kind of risque for mixed company?

10:03.000 --> 10:05.000
I don't think so.

10:05.000 --> 10:09.000
They were just singing and drinking a little wine, but they're out in the wilderness.

10:09.000 --> 10:12.000
True. They could be married, it don't say.

10:12.000 --> 10:15.000
A man don't go out in the woods with his wife.

10:15.000 --> 10:18.000
Yeah, but he was drinking wine, don't forget.

10:20.000 --> 10:22.000
That's enough discussion.

10:22.000 --> 10:25.000
Um, who else has some poetry?

10:25.000 --> 10:27.000
You, Bobby-Joe?

10:27.000 --> 10:30.000
Oh, I'd rather just sit and listen, Ma.

10:32.000 --> 10:35.000
How about you fellas? Don't you have some poetry you like?

10:35.000 --> 10:38.000
Here's a little poem I kind of like.

10:38.000 --> 10:41.000
I never seen a purple cow.

10:41.000 --> 10:43.000
I never hoped to see one.

10:43.000 --> 10:47.000
But I can tell you anyhow, I'd rather see than be one.

10:51.000 --> 10:53.000
Don't you get it?

10:53.000 --> 10:56.000
The fella said he'd rather see a purple cow than be a purple cow.

10:56.000 --> 11:00.000
Well, naturally. Who'd want to be a purple cow?

11:01.000 --> 11:04.000
Come to think about that, that ain't so funny.

11:04.000 --> 11:06.000
I know a funny one.

11:06.000 --> 11:09.000
There was a young girl from Duluth.

11:09.000 --> 11:13.000
I think the time has come for our guest of honor,

11:13.000 --> 11:16.000
Mr. Alan Landman of New York City,

11:16.000 --> 11:19.000
to treat us to a sample of real poetry.

11:22.000 --> 11:25.000
I'd be happy to take part in this gig,

11:25.000 --> 11:29.000
although I don't know whether I'm far enough out for you cats or not.

11:29.000 --> 11:33.000
This is my latest poem.

11:33.000 --> 11:37.000
A gut-thin whining wind

11:37.000 --> 11:42.000
defiles the groaning bones of neon-blinded seekers

11:42.000 --> 11:45.000
after murky morning.

11:45.000 --> 11:48.000
The burning mud explodes

11:48.000 --> 11:51.000
a screaming pathway

11:51.000 --> 11:54.000
to hollow thunder of agony.

11:54.000 --> 11:58.000
I fall, I fall,

11:58.000 --> 12:01.000
and cheated dreams

12:01.000 --> 12:04.000
a toenail splits.

12:12.000 --> 12:15.000
Who would like to start the discussion?

12:15.000 --> 12:19.000
I think that was one of the most exciting poems I've ever heard.

12:19.000 --> 12:21.000
Anybody else?

12:21.000 --> 12:23.000
Floyd?

12:23.000 --> 12:27.000
Did you have an emotional reaction from Mr. Landman's poem?

12:27.000 --> 12:30.000
Yeah, but I think it is Dutch apple pie.

12:30.000 --> 12:33.000
I'd like to know what it means, if anything.

12:33.000 --> 12:36.000
So would I.

12:37.000 --> 12:41.000
It means that this is a cemetery,

12:41.000 --> 12:43.000
and you're all corpses.

12:43.000 --> 12:45.000
Good night.

12:47.000 --> 12:49.000
Oh, Alan, wait for me!

12:52.000 --> 12:53.000
Want a cigarette?

12:53.000 --> 12:55.000
Sure.

12:55.000 --> 12:59.000
I'll be right back.

13:05.000 --> 13:07.000
The wind.

13:07.000 --> 13:09.000
The wind's kind of chilly.

13:09.000 --> 13:11.000
The wind is kind of chilly?

13:11.000 --> 13:14.000
Naughty-Eye Boyer says about the wind.

13:14.000 --> 13:16.000
What?

13:16.000 --> 13:18.000
The wind is a switchblade knife

13:18.000 --> 13:23.000
slashing at the polluting smokestacks of urban insanity.

13:23.000 --> 13:28.000
A number with a defiling beetle called man.

13:28.000 --> 13:31.000
I wish I could learn to express myself like that.

13:31.000 --> 13:35.000
Well, you'll never do it around here, baby.

13:36.000 --> 13:38.000
So long. I'm going for a walk.

13:38.000 --> 13:41.000
Can I come along? Please?

13:41.000 --> 13:44.000
I mean, I won't talk. I'll just listen.

13:44.000 --> 13:54.000
Okay, kid.

14:15.000 --> 14:17.000
Kate, Kate.

14:17.000 --> 14:19.000
What? Who's sleeping?

14:19.000 --> 14:21.000
It's one o'clock. Why don't you go on up to bed?

14:21.000 --> 14:23.000
I'll wait up for Bobby-Jo and that poet.

14:23.000 --> 14:28.000
No. I want to see Bobby-Jo when she comes in.

14:28.000 --> 14:31.000
Well, in that case...

14:36.000 --> 14:39.000
I don't think Bobby-Jo's really serious about that.

14:39.000 --> 14:44.000
When I was her age, I nearly ran away with a boy just like him.

14:44.000 --> 14:46.000
That's what worries me.

14:46.000 --> 14:48.000
Yeah, but Bobby-Jo...

14:48.000 --> 14:50.000
Here they come.

15:00.000 --> 15:02.000
Oh, what a terrible hand.

15:02.000 --> 15:04.000
I ought to shoot you, Uncle.

15:04.000 --> 15:11.000
For giving me cards like this.

15:11.000 --> 15:12.000
Oh, look who's here.

15:12.000 --> 15:13.000
Sorry we're late.

15:13.000 --> 15:15.000
Late? Is it late?

15:15.000 --> 15:17.000
I didn't notice.

15:17.000 --> 15:20.000
Mom, I know you're waiting up for me.

15:20.000 --> 15:22.000
Yes, I was.

15:22.000 --> 15:25.000
Go to bed, honey. I want to talk to Mr. Landman.

15:25.000 --> 15:27.000
Okay, but I want you to know that whatever he says,

15:27.000 --> 15:30.000
I'm completely on his side.

15:30.000 --> 15:32.000
Good night, Ellen.

15:32.000 --> 15:34.000
Good night, baby.

15:34.000 --> 15:36.000
Good night, Uncle Joe.

15:36.000 --> 15:37.000
Am I leaving?

15:37.000 --> 15:39.000
Yes, you are.

15:43.000 --> 15:44.000
Good night, Pops.

15:44.000 --> 15:48.000
Uncle Joe, Mr. Landman said good night to you.

15:48.000 --> 15:52.000
He can't expect a corpse to answer you back,

15:52.000 --> 15:55.000
especially when his name isn't Pops.

15:55.000 --> 15:58.000
Okay, I'm going to save you a lot of time.

15:58.000 --> 16:00.000
I'm crazy about Bobby.

16:00.000 --> 16:03.000
We've been talking about me sending for her

16:03.000 --> 16:05.000
when I get to New Orleans.

16:05.000 --> 16:07.000
Uh-huh.

16:07.000 --> 16:10.000
Is that all you're going to do, just sit there and smile?

16:10.000 --> 16:13.000
Smiling on the outside, screaming on the inside.

16:13.000 --> 16:15.000
Tell me something, Mr. Landman.

16:15.000 --> 16:17.000
In view of the circumstances,

16:17.000 --> 16:19.000
don't you think you should call me?

16:19.000 --> 16:21.000
I don't know what you're talking about.

16:21.000 --> 16:23.000
I'm not going to call you.

16:23.000 --> 16:25.000
In view of the circumstances,

16:25.000 --> 16:27.000
don't you think you should call me Allen?

16:27.000 --> 16:31.000
In view of the circumstances, what I should call you,

16:31.000 --> 16:34.000
but I'll settle for Mr. Landman.

16:34.000 --> 16:37.000
Are you planning on getting a job when you go to New Orleans?

16:37.000 --> 16:39.000
Are you kidding?

16:39.000 --> 16:41.000
I mean, work is for suckers.

16:41.000 --> 16:43.000
Oh, I see, I see.

16:43.000 --> 16:45.000
How are you going to live?

16:45.000 --> 16:48.000
Oh, I've got a lot of friends down there, musicians.

16:48.000 --> 16:50.000
They all like me.

16:50.000 --> 16:52.000
They all have pads.

16:52.000 --> 16:54.000
They all have rooms.

16:54.000 --> 16:56.000
Thank you.

16:56.000 --> 17:00.000
Then you plan on living your life as a parasite.

17:00.000 --> 17:02.000
That means sponge.

17:02.000 --> 17:06.000
Listen, this is strictly nowhere.

17:06.000 --> 17:09.000
You've got a lot of sick, old-fashioned ideas

17:09.000 --> 17:11.000
about working hard and living clean,

17:11.000 --> 17:13.000
and it's all a lot of junk.

17:13.000 --> 17:15.000
I'm too tired to listen.

17:15.000 --> 17:17.000
Now, Bobby knows what she's getting into.

17:17.000 --> 17:19.000
It sounds good to her.

17:19.000 --> 17:22.000
I'll see you in the morning. Good night.

17:28.000 --> 17:30.000
You heard that?

17:30.000 --> 17:32.000
Yeah, it's bad. What are you going to do?

17:32.000 --> 17:34.000
I don't know.

17:34.000 --> 17:36.000
Of course, you realize he'll be gone tomorrow.

17:36.000 --> 17:38.000
He'll never send for her. You know that.

17:38.000 --> 17:40.000
I know that.

17:40.000 --> 17:43.000
And she'll always remember him as a romantic young rebel

17:43.000 --> 17:46.000
full of courage and exciting ideas.

17:46.000 --> 17:48.000
Yeah, I guess you're right.

17:48.000 --> 17:50.000
Why don't you have a good talk with her?

17:50.000 --> 17:52.000
That's the worst thing I could do.

17:52.000 --> 17:54.000
We'd only wind up yelling at each other,

17:54.000 --> 17:56.000
and he'd be a bigger hero than ever to her.

17:56.000 --> 17:59.000
No, talking to her about it would be a big mistake.

17:59.000 --> 18:01.000
Big mistake.

18:02.000 --> 18:05.000
All those ideas of his are just a cover-up

18:05.000 --> 18:07.000
and an excuse for being a failure,

18:07.000 --> 18:10.000
for not having a job, for just being a bum,

18:10.000 --> 18:13.000
wandering around and living off other people.

18:13.000 --> 18:16.000
And you have been taken in by all that phony talk,

18:16.000 --> 18:19.000
and I am making a big mistake.

18:19.000 --> 18:22.000
Oh, Mom, you just don't understand Alan.

18:22.000 --> 18:25.000
He has ideals.

18:25.000 --> 18:27.000
He thinks the world is full of hypocrisy,

18:27.000 --> 18:29.000
and he's attacking it.

18:29.000 --> 18:31.000
That's a lot of hogwash.

18:31.000 --> 18:34.000
He's just afraid to go out and fight for a job.

18:34.000 --> 18:37.000
But just let somebody offer him a nice, soft position,

18:37.000 --> 18:41.000
and you'll see how fast he'll stop attacking that hypocrisy

18:41.000 --> 18:43.000
and start lapping it up.

18:43.000 --> 18:46.000
And now that I've made my big mistake, good night.

18:46.000 --> 18:53.000
♪

18:53.000 --> 18:58.000
♪

18:58.000 --> 19:01.000
♪

19:01.000 --> 19:04.000
Your mother's just looking out for your welfare, Bobby-Joe.

19:04.000 --> 19:07.000
I suppose you don't like Alan either.

19:07.000 --> 19:09.000
No, but it's nothing personal.

19:09.000 --> 19:10.000
He's been nice to me.

19:10.000 --> 19:13.000
In fact, he told me I was the biggest square shooter he'd ever met.

19:13.000 --> 19:16.000
Of course, he took a poetic license and left out shooter.

19:16.000 --> 19:19.000
Oh, Bobby-Joe.

19:19.000 --> 19:20.000
Thank you, Mrs. Bradley.

19:20.000 --> 19:23.000
Oh, Uncle Joe, I'd like you to meet Mr. Stanley.

19:23.000 --> 19:25.000
He's going to spend a couple of days with us,

19:25.000 --> 19:27.000
get away from the high pressure of big business.

19:27.000 --> 19:28.000
Hi. Hello there.

19:28.000 --> 19:29.000
This is my daughter, Bobby-Joe.

19:29.000 --> 19:31.000
Hello, Mrs. Bradley.

19:31.000 --> 19:32.000
You're leaving, Alan?

19:32.000 --> 19:34.000
Yeah, kid, but you'll be hearing from me.

19:34.000 --> 19:37.000
Oh, Mr. Landman, you should meet Mr. Stanley.

19:37.000 --> 19:39.000
He's from New York, too.

19:39.000 --> 19:40.000
Oh, Mr. Stanley, Mr. Landman.

19:40.000 --> 19:41.000
How are you, young man?

19:41.000 --> 19:43.000
You really want to know?

19:43.000 --> 19:46.000
Mr. Stanley is the president of the Rallo Dog Food Company.

19:46.000 --> 19:49.000
He was telling me all about his advertising problems.

19:49.000 --> 19:51.000
It's driving me out of my mind.

19:51.000 --> 19:55.000
I've offered a $2,000 bonus for a jingle I can use on radio and television,

19:55.000 --> 19:58.000
and no one in my agency can come up with one.

19:58.000 --> 20:01.000
$2,000 just for a jingle?

20:01.000 --> 20:03.000
Just a four-line jingle.

20:03.000 --> 20:05.000
Mr. Landman is a poet.

20:05.000 --> 20:07.000
Oh, of course, he only writes iClass stuff.

20:07.000 --> 20:09.000
Is that so?

20:09.000 --> 20:11.000
$2,000.

20:13.000 --> 20:15.000
I know what's going on in your mind.

20:15.000 --> 20:17.000
You do?

20:17.000 --> 20:19.000
You think Alan's going to be tempted by that $2,000

20:19.000 --> 20:22.000
and try to sell Mr. Stanley a dog food jingle.

20:22.000 --> 20:24.000
Oh.

20:24.000 --> 20:27.000
I don't think a superior artist full of ideals would do that.

20:27.000 --> 20:29.000
No, never.

20:29.000 --> 20:33.000
By the way, I thought your poet friend was leaving this morning.

20:33.000 --> 20:35.000
Well, he was, but he had to...

20:35.000 --> 20:37.000
Listen to this one, Mr. Stanley.

20:37.000 --> 20:41.000
Rallo dog food is never gummy.

20:41.000 --> 20:45.000
Can't form a ball in your puppy's tummy.

20:45.000 --> 20:49.000
Rallo dog food is doggone yummy.

20:49.000 --> 20:52.000
Oh, it has human appeal,

20:52.000 --> 20:54.000
but speaking from the dog's viewpoint,

20:54.000 --> 20:57.000
it doesn't make my tail wag.

20:57.000 --> 20:59.000
How about this one?

20:59.000 --> 21:02.000
When your poodle's feeling hollow,

21:02.000 --> 21:05.000
fill him up with a can of Rallo.

21:10.000 --> 21:13.000
What's so wrong with Alan wanting to earn $2,000

21:13.000 --> 21:15.000
when you think about it?

21:15.000 --> 21:17.000
Nothing at all.

21:17.000 --> 21:19.000
It'll give him the freedom to write real poetry.

21:19.000 --> 21:21.000
Absolutely.

21:21.000 --> 21:24.000
Besides, if you have a genuine big talent like Alan does,

21:24.000 --> 21:26.000
you can write those silly jingles

21:26.000 --> 21:28.000
without losing any of your artistic dignity.

21:28.000 --> 21:30.000
He never loses dignity.

21:30.000 --> 21:32.000
His talent's too big.

21:32.000 --> 21:36.000
Get some Rallo now, now, now.

21:36.000 --> 21:38.000
Make your dog say,

21:38.000 --> 21:40.000
bow, wow, wow.

21:40.000 --> 21:42.000
How's that for real dog appeal?

21:42.000 --> 21:44.000
Does that make your tail wag, Mr. Stanley?

21:44.000 --> 21:46.000
Mr. Landman, I'll be honest.

21:46.000 --> 21:48.000
You have talent, all right,

21:48.000 --> 21:51.000
but I just don't think you talk dog language.

21:51.000 --> 21:53.000
Forgive me for saying it, sir,

21:53.000 --> 21:56.000
but must your advertising appeal to the dogs?

21:56.000 --> 21:58.000
I mean, after all, it is the humans who buy it.

21:58.000 --> 22:00.000
We've got to please the dog.

22:00.000 --> 22:02.000
We cater to the dog.

22:02.000 --> 22:05.000
We've got to sleep dog, eat dog, think dog.

22:05.000 --> 22:07.000
Do you think I'm crazy?

22:07.000 --> 22:09.000
Oh, crazy, no, sir.

22:09.000 --> 22:11.000
Oh, I know. You're after the money.

22:11.000 --> 22:13.000
Well, I'll pay anything to the writer

22:13.000 --> 22:16.000
who'll get inside the dog's mind with me.

22:16.000 --> 22:18.000
I'm in there, but I can't write.

22:18.000 --> 22:20.000
You really think I could do it, given the little time?

22:20.000 --> 22:22.000
Well, I don't know.

22:22.000 --> 22:24.000
Dog persons are born, not made.

22:24.000 --> 22:26.000
You've either got that dog in you or you haven't,

22:26.000 --> 22:28.000
and you can't please a dog consistently

22:28.000 --> 22:30.000
if you don't have the dog's viewpoint.

22:30.000 --> 22:32.000
I feel I really have it, sir.

22:32.000 --> 22:34.000
I don't know. You're tempted by the money.

22:34.000 --> 22:36.000
No, really.

22:36.000 --> 22:40.000
Dogs, they seem to feel that I'm one of them.

22:40.000 --> 22:42.000
They accept me.

22:42.000 --> 22:44.000
Are you sure?

22:44.000 --> 22:47.000
Sometimes they pretend just to get food out of you.

22:47.000 --> 22:49.000
No, no, it's sincere.

22:49.000 --> 22:52.000
I warn you, you can't fool a real dog person,

22:52.000 --> 22:55.000
and I'm real dog, my boy.

22:55.000 --> 22:57.000
I'll tell you something confidential, may I?

22:57.000 --> 22:59.000
Please.

22:59.000 --> 23:03.000
When I see a cat, I want to chase it up a tree.

23:03.000 --> 23:05.000
Now you think I'm crazy, right?

23:05.000 --> 23:07.000
No, on the contrary.

23:07.000 --> 23:11.000
I've never told anyone, but I like to run after cars

23:11.000 --> 23:15.000
and bite at the tires.

23:15.000 --> 23:17.000
Well, it's a good sign.

23:17.000 --> 23:19.000
You're not lying.

23:19.000 --> 23:22.000
I'd cross my heart and hope to get run over.

23:22.000 --> 23:26.000
This may be a great day for all of us.

23:26.000 --> 23:28.000
I'd like to shake your paw.

23:28.000 --> 23:30.000
Bow-wow-wow!

23:30.000 --> 23:32.000
Woof, woof, woof!

23:32.000 --> 23:34.000
Woof, woof, woof!

23:34.000 --> 23:36.000
Woof, woof, woof!

23:36.000 --> 23:38.000
Woof, woof, woof!

23:38.000 --> 23:41.000
Poor Bobby Jo, she sure is busted up.

23:41.000 --> 23:44.000
I wish somebody'd invent a way to take the pain out of growing up.

23:44.000 --> 23:46.000
Boy, I can't get over how lucky it was,

23:46.000 --> 23:49.000
that crazy dog food man showing up here just in time

23:49.000 --> 23:51.000
to show up that Mr. Landman.

23:51.000 --> 23:52.000
Isn't that lucky, Doc?

23:52.000 --> 23:54.000
It was amazing.

23:54.000 --> 23:56.000
You might call it fate.

23:56.000 --> 23:57.000
You might.

23:57.000 --> 24:00.000
That's fate spelled K-A-T-E.

24:00.000 --> 24:01.000
What's going on here?

24:01.000 --> 24:03.000
Uncle Joe met Roger Stanley,

24:03.000 --> 24:05.000
the best restaurant supply salesman in the country.

24:05.000 --> 24:07.000
Restaurant supply?

24:07.000 --> 24:08.000
He's new in this territory.

24:08.000 --> 24:10.000
First time he was here, you were in Bixley with the girls.

24:10.000 --> 24:12.000
Glad I could help you out, Kate.

24:12.000 --> 24:15.000
Oh, you put him up to the whole thing.

24:15.000 --> 24:18.000
You did a wonderful job, Roger.

24:18.000 --> 24:22.000
And any time, you are my guest here for free.

24:22.000 --> 24:24.000
Oh, forget it.

24:24.000 --> 24:25.000
I had a lot of fun.

24:25.000 --> 24:27.000
I have a dirty trick on that kid, though.

24:27.000 --> 24:30.000
Oh, it'll do him more good than harm.

24:30.000 --> 24:31.000
Well, so long.

24:31.000 --> 24:32.000
I'll see you the next time through.

24:32.000 --> 24:33.000
Bye, Roger.

24:33.000 --> 24:34.000
Thanks again.

24:34.000 --> 24:35.000
And don't chase any cats.

24:35.000 --> 24:36.000
Ruff!

24:36.000 --> 24:37.000
Ruff!

24:37.000 --> 24:40.000
So...

24:40.000 --> 24:42.000
You know, Kate, I never realized it,

24:42.000 --> 24:44.000
but you're a gall dang genius.

24:44.000 --> 24:46.000
Oh, no, Uncle Joe.

24:46.000 --> 24:49.000
I'm just a gall dang mother.

25:16.000 --> 25:20.000
This has been a Filmways presentation.

